1
00:00:09,318 --> 00:00:13,470
Now, let's talk about filtering and
address translation.

2
00:00:15,650 --> 00:00:20,655
So filtering, with filtering,
we want to block or

3
00:00:20,655 --> 00:00:24,838
allow traffic according to some policy.

4
00:00:24,838 --> 00:00:28,080
So maybe, we're running a web server and

5
00:00:28,080 --> 00:00:31,842
we want to block all traffic,
not for port 80.

6
00:00:31,842 --> 00:00:39,362
So port 80 would be what you would
commonly have open for HTTP requests.

7
00:00:39,362 --> 00:00:44,288
So maybe, let's just protect
that web server to not allow any

8
00:00:44,288 --> 00:00:47,310
other traffic to come in.

9
00:00:47,310 --> 00:00:52,491
Maybe we're running a home gateway,
where we want to block

10
00:00:52,491 --> 00:00:58,298
all traffic except for
connections initiated from internal.

11
00:00:58,298 --> 00:01:02,570
So this is a nice security
mechanism from the home

12
00:01:02,570 --> 00:01:06,804
where no external requests
can come through.

13
00:01:06,804 --> 00:01:12,327
Unless I went to some website,
initiated that connection,

14
00:01:12,327 --> 00:01:16,280
I will let that website respond, right?

15
00:01:16,280 --> 00:01:20,226
And so,
this can be at the edge of some network,

16
00:01:20,226 --> 00:01:24,730
but it could also be on
the end hosts themselves.

17
00:01:24,730 --> 00:01:31,290
The mechanisms are going to be the same,
but where you apply it may differ.

18
00:01:31,290 --> 00:01:37,794
So the core of all of this
is match action tables.

19
00:01:37,794 --> 00:01:45,131
So the match, which sometimes you
can also hear called classification,

20
00:01:45,131 --> 00:01:53,650
it compares the input packets to a rule
set in a table to see if there's a match.

21
00:01:53,650 --> 00:01:59,291
So for example, we're going to
match against the destination

22
00:01:59,291 --> 00:02:06,238
IP address of 1.2.3.0/24 and
destination port equals 80.

23
00:02:06,238 --> 00:02:12,434
And in this table it says, we're
going to drop that particular traffic.

24
00:02:12,434 --> 00:02:17,217
So if you recall,
the general kind of packet processing

25
00:02:17,217 --> 00:02:21,897
pipeline is receive a packet,
look up in some tables,

26
00:02:21,897 --> 00:02:26,736
and do some manipulation of
the packets based on that.

27
00:02:26,736 --> 00:02:28,624
This is going to be very similar.

28
00:02:28,624 --> 00:02:32,823
So the matches look up or
kind of rules in a table, and

29
00:02:32,823 --> 00:02:37,596
then, the actions tell us what
we want to do with the packet,

30
00:02:37,596 --> 00:02:41,520
maybe we want to drop it,
maybe we want to allow it.

31
00:02:43,650 --> 00:02:49,530
So a good practice to follow
would be a default drop.

32
00:02:49,530 --> 00:02:53,765
So you set the default action to drop,
so if there's no matches,

33
00:02:53,765 --> 00:02:56,414
then you're going to drop the traffic.

34
00:02:56,414 --> 00:03:01,590
And then, you add rules to have
an action other than drop.

35
00:03:01,590 --> 00:03:06,482
So this might be commonly called
something like whitelisting.

36
00:03:06,482 --> 00:03:11,050
So you have the rules for what you're
going to allow into your network

37
00:03:11,050 --> 00:03:13,670
rather than what you want to disallow.

38
00:03:16,090 --> 00:03:19,860
So that's filtering,
it's a pretty easy concept,

39
00:03:19,860 --> 00:03:24,430
and we're going to learn about
iptables and how to apply it.

40
00:03:24,430 --> 00:03:29,810
On the complementary side
is address translation.

41
00:03:29,810 --> 00:03:34,561
So, actions can also
manipulate the addresses or

42
00:03:34,561 --> 00:03:38,010
different fields in the packets.

43
00:03:38,010 --> 00:03:42,961
So here we're talking about source and
destination IP addresses and

44
00:03:42,961 --> 00:03:47,352
source and destination ports,
so like TCP port numbers.

45
00:03:47,352 --> 00:03:52,357
So an example of this is,
maybe I want to use private addressing on

46
00:03:52,357 --> 00:03:58,170
the local area network or LAN with
a single shared public IP address.

47
00:03:58,170 --> 00:04:05,020
So packets destined to the public IP
address are translated to a private one.

48
00:04:07,070 --> 00:04:15,318
All right, so let's go through a few
use cases of this address translation.

49
00:04:15,318 --> 00:04:19,906
So the first use case is,
maybe I have [COUGH] performing

50
00:04:19,906 --> 00:04:24,370
a static mapping from
internal to external.

51
00:04:24,370 --> 00:04:29,844
Right, so each server in my network
will get both a public IP address and

52
00:04:29,844 --> 00:04:31,768
a private IP address.

53
00:04:31,768 --> 00:04:35,406
So when it's communicating internally,

54
00:04:35,406 --> 00:04:41,265
it's used in this private IP address,
but when it goes externally,

55
00:04:41,265 --> 00:04:46,431
this gets translated to some
public IP address, all right?

56
00:04:46,431 --> 00:04:49,548
And there's different reasons
you might want to do this.

57
00:04:49,548 --> 00:04:56,115
Maybe you have some kind of internal
legacy management system set up.

58
00:04:56,115 --> 00:05:00,938
Or maybe this is how you set up
your LAN to be able to handle this,

59
00:05:00,938 --> 00:05:05,230
but you wanted to then
grant them external access.

60
00:05:05,230 --> 00:05:10,292
So here, let's start with kind of how
to read these diagrams that I drew up.

61
00:05:10,292 --> 00:05:14,972
Well, so this is showing
the packet as it's traversing from

62
00:05:14,972 --> 00:05:19,743
the internal to the external,
and we have source IP address,

63
00:05:19,743 --> 00:05:24,445
destination IP address,
source port, destination port.

64
00:05:24,445 --> 00:05:27,840
And so, that's coming from this host and

65
00:05:27,840 --> 00:05:32,702
it's going to some public IP address,
some destination.

66
00:05:32,702 --> 00:05:39,460
At the gateway, it's going to translate,
do it have a static translation?

67
00:05:39,460 --> 00:05:44,548
Recall, we have a mapping,
and so, it's going to say,

68
00:05:44,548 --> 00:05:50,635
if I see in ingress or
in the egress direction 10.0.0.1,

69
00:05:50,635 --> 00:05:54,307
translate the source to this address.

70
00:05:54,307 --> 00:06:01,088
And in the ingress direction, translate
the destination from this to this.

71
00:06:01,088 --> 00:06:05,146
So you can see it was
translated externally.

72
00:06:05,146 --> 00:06:09,451
So now, when that server responds,
it'll have its source, but

73
00:06:09,451 --> 00:06:12,952
then the destination will
be that public address.

74
00:06:12,952 --> 00:06:13,745
And then,

75
00:06:13,745 --> 00:06:19,990
we're going to do a static mapping to
the destination internal IP address.

76
00:06:19,990 --> 00:06:25,242
So that's one example,
might not be commonly used, but so,

77
00:06:25,242 --> 00:06:32,730
this one use case 2, you certainly would
commonly experience this or see this.

78
00:06:32,730 --> 00:06:38,135
So this might be a dynamic mapping
of a single external IP address,

79
00:06:38,135 --> 00:06:42,970
and then you dynamically select
from available TCP ports.

80
00:06:45,630 --> 00:06:48,362
Internally, we have a bunch
of different hosts.

81
00:06:48,362 --> 00:06:52,424
They all get private IP addresses and

82
00:06:52,424 --> 00:06:56,354
we have assigned, as the gateway,

83
00:06:56,354 --> 00:07:02,630
a single public IP address for
this entire network.

84
00:07:02,630 --> 00:07:05,624
So to be able to distinguish
the different hosts,

85
00:07:05,624 --> 00:07:09,670
it's going to use the port
numbers as part of this process.

86
00:07:09,670 --> 00:07:14,570
So here again, we're going to
have a request going externally.

87
00:07:14,570 --> 00:07:19,143
So the source would be 10.0.1.1,
destination,

88
00:07:19,143 --> 00:07:24,461
the same kind of public IP address
that we're sending a request to,

89
00:07:24,461 --> 00:07:27,734
and our source and destination ports.

90
00:07:27,734 --> 00:07:30,195
So once it gets to the gateway,

91
00:07:30,195 --> 00:07:34,848
it's going to look up in its table and
see if it has a mapping.

92
00:07:34,848 --> 00:07:38,202
In this case, it does have a mapping.

93
00:07:38,202 --> 00:07:43,322
It's saying, 10.0.1.1/5000 will

94
00:07:43,322 --> 00:07:47,971
map to port 1000 externally, all right?

95
00:07:47,971 --> 00:07:52,082
And it'll include also
the destination IP address,

96
00:07:52,082 --> 00:07:57,300
as well as part of that, but for
space first purposes left that off.

97
00:07:58,550 --> 00:08:03,270
All right, and so, now, you can see,

98
00:08:03,270 --> 00:08:11,210
both the source end IP and
TCP port and address got translated.

99
00:08:11,210 --> 00:08:18,491
So now, when this guy responds to
the destination of that single IP address,

100
00:08:18,491 --> 00:08:22,532
that all hosts in this network will share.

101
00:08:22,532 --> 00:08:27,658
Again, it'll look up in a table,
and it will see,

102
00:08:27,658 --> 00:08:34,490
it should translate it to this internal
IP address port number mapping.

103
00:08:34,490 --> 00:08:38,387
And it will do that, and so,
that it can get to the destination.

104
00:08:38,387 --> 00:08:43,714
All right, now, if it was the first
request, let's say another host comes

105
00:08:43,714 --> 00:08:48,734
in and has a similar request,
it would look up, there's no mapping.

106
00:08:48,734 --> 00:08:53,314
Well, it would just pick
one of the available TCP

107
00:08:53,314 --> 00:08:56,900
ports to have as part of that mapping.

108
00:08:56,900 --> 00:08:59,404
All right, so that's a second use case.

109
00:08:59,404 --> 00:09:06,010
And so, why I say this is common is, a lot
of home networks have this exact setup.

110
00:09:07,790 --> 00:09:11,718
A third case might be port forwarding.

111
00:09:11,718 --> 00:09:18,628
So let's say, I want to run a publicly
available server on a private IP address.

112
00:09:18,628 --> 00:09:22,856
So say I am into Minecraft in my home and

113
00:09:22,856 --> 00:09:29,209
I want to run a server in a server or
on a computer in my home.

114
00:09:29,209 --> 00:09:32,158
Well, I'm going to get
a private IP address, but

115
00:09:32,158 --> 00:09:36,583
I also have a bunch of other family
members that are going to have other kind

116
00:09:36,583 --> 00:09:39,483
of computers that
are using the same network.

117
00:09:39,483 --> 00:09:45,300
So we all get private IP addresses, and
then, we get this single IP address for

118
00:09:45,300 --> 00:09:49,388
the whole network, but
I want to make this available.

119
00:09:49,388 --> 00:09:53,436
So this is where port forwarding comes in.

120
00:09:53,436 --> 00:09:58,455
So requests come in, so now,
we're looking from left to

121
00:09:58,455 --> 00:10:04,670
right as the first request to
the destination of this gateway.

122
00:10:04,670 --> 00:10:09,791
But then, the gateway knows,
all right, if I see port 25565,

123
00:10:09,791 --> 00:10:14,824
I'm going to forward it to
the internal server 10.0., whoops,

124
00:10:14,824 --> 00:10:20,926
this should say 1.1, which would be
where I'm running my Minecraft server.

125
00:10:20,926 --> 00:10:24,305
And then,
it will do the address translation,

126
00:10:24,305 --> 00:10:29,626
likewise on the reverse direction,
when the Minecraft server responds,

127
00:10:29,626 --> 00:10:34,010
it knows that there's
going to be this translation.

128
00:10:34,010 --> 00:10:37,100
So that's what port forwarding does.

129
00:10:37,100 --> 00:10:42,136
And you can see,
each of these are kind of distinguished

130
00:10:42,136 --> 00:10:47,080
by what fields are getting translated and
all of that.

131
00:10:49,210 --> 00:10:55,740
All right, so that's the basics for what
filtering and address translation are.

132
00:10:55,740 --> 00:11:01,650
Next, we're going to dive into
the iptables and how to use that.

133
00:11:01,650 --> 00:11:03,690
All right, till then.